H1("I. INTRODUCTION")
BP("The global proliferation of sovereign data privacy legislation has transformed regulatory compliance into a critical challenge. Following Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India, Parliament enacted the Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023. Under Section 3(b), the law asserts explicit extraterritorial jurisdiction: any digital personal data processing conducted outside India, if connected with offering goods or services to Indian data principals, falls within statutory scope. Consequently, global websites worldwide are legally bound by India's sovereign data protection regime.")
BP("A fiduciary's privacy policy constitutes the binding statutory contract. A PwC India survey of 3,233 consumers revealed only 16% were aware of the DPDP Act, and 69% did not know they could withdraw consent. Global entities deploy monolithic policies for GDPR or US safe harbors that systematically breach Indian mandates: omitting notices in 22 Eighth Schedule languages (S.5(3)); imposing friction on consent withdrawal (S.6(4)); asserting perpetual data retention (S.8(7)); tracking minors without parental consent (S.9); and omitting an accessible Indian DPO (S.13).")
BP("Language models present an intuitive auditing mechanism. However, Dahl et al. [1] demonstrated LLM hallucination rates between 58-88% on verifiable legal questions. General LLMs exhibit jurisdictional bias, superimposing GDPR 'legitimate interests' onto an Indian statute with a closed Section 7 list [2]. A third critical failure is treating contractual silence as affirmative violation: if a fiduciary does not engage in cross-border transfers, it has no duty to mention Section 16, yet naive models flag such omissions as non-compliance.")
H2("A. Limits of Existing Work")
BP("APLiance (Dhakar, Sinha and Sural [3]) formalises Sections 1-7 as ABAC rules and uses GPT-5.4-mini to extract 16 attributes, reporting 94.5% macro-accuracy across 25 policies. However, APLiance shows only 64% accuracy on unambiguous consent, an ultra-conservative silence penalty causing 100% of 25 real-world policies to fail compliance, and no serving infrastructure. InLegalBERT [4] and Aalap [5] target retrospective litigation, not real-time DPDP contract compliance.")
H2("B. Key Contributions")
BP("Ssense makes six primary contributions: (C1) First Open-Weights DPDP Law-LM: dual-track architecture with forensic auditor (r=128) and citizen dialogue (r=64) adapters via rsLoRA + SimPO, released as Ssense-DPDP-7B. (C2) Joint Computational Formalization: 26-node statutory violation taxonomy with deterministic penalty weights omega in {30, 20, 10, 5}. (C3) Epistemic Omission Firewall: three-step deductive chain ensuring unstated practices are not penalized unless an affirmative statutory duty of disclosure exists. (C4) Lossless Neuro-Symbolic Backstop: V_final = V_SLM union V_SCL with safe-harbor lookarounds eliminating false negatives on long-context policies. (C5) High-Throughput Inference: PagedAttention (B up to 16), XGrammar pushdown CFG, 1.8x speculative speedup, five-tier SHA-256 cache hierarchy. (C6) 18-Axis Algorithmic Certification: bipartite multi-violation F1, McNemar tests, 95% Wilson lower bounds.")
H1("II. BACKGROUND AND PRELIMINARIES")
H2("A. Statutory Architecture: Deontic Modalities of the DPDP Act, 2023")
BP("India's DPDP Act, 2023 is formalizable as M_statute = {O (Obligations), P (Prohibitions), Perm (Legitimate Uses), S (Safe Harbors)}. Obligations: itemized notices in 22 Eighth Schedule languages (S.5(1), S.5(3)); consent withdrawal as easy as giving (S.6(4)); reasonable security safeguards (S.8(5)); erasure upon purpose completion (S.8(7)). Prohibitions: bundled consent (S.6(1)); child tracking (S.9(2)-(3)); cross-border transfers to non-notified territories (S.16). Critically, Section 7 provides a closed, exhaustive list of nine legitimate uses -- Indian law recognizes no broad commercial 'legitimate interest' unlike GDPR.")
TC("TABLE I: DPDP Statutory Provisions, Deontic Modalities, and Global Web Defects")
t=doc.add_table(rows=1,cols=4); t.style="Table Grid"; t.alignment=WD_TABLE_ALIGNMENT.CENTER
TH(t,["Act Section & Rule","Deontic Modality","Statutory Mandate","Typical Global Web Defect"])
TR(t,[
    ["S.3(b)","Extraterritoriality","Extraterritorial scope over global entities","Assumes local laws inapplicable; generic US/EU policy"],
    ["S.5(1), S.5(3), Rule 3","Obligation (O)","Itemized notice in 22 Eighth Schedule languages","Monolithic English text; vague categories; no language notice"],
    ["S.6(1), S.6(4), Rule 4","Obligation + Prohibition","Consent free, specific, informed; withdrawal as easy as giving","Bundled consent; dark patterns; withdrawal by postal mail"],
    ["S.7","Closed Permission","Closed list of 9 legitimate uses; commercial profiling excluded","Asserts broad GDPR-style 'legitimate interest'"],
    ["S.8(5), S.8(7), Rule 6, 8","Obligation (O)","Reasonable safeguards; erase data once purpose fulfilled","Perpetual retention; no technical safeguards disclosed"],
    ["S.9(1)-(3), Rule 10","Prohibition + Obligation","Verifiable parental consent; absolute ban on child tracking","Indiscriminate ad-network tracking on minor-accessible platforms"],
    ["S.13, Rule 11","Obligation (O)","Accessible grievance redressal with Indian DPO contact","Omits Indian grievance officer; offshore legal teams"],
    ["S.16, Rule 13(4), Sched.2","Prohibition (P)","Restrict cross-border transfers to non-notified territories","Unrestricted data transfer clauses to offshore affiliates"],
])
FT(t)
H2("B. Parameter-Efficient Alignment: rsLoRA and SimPO")
BP("We build upon Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct [12], a decoder-only Transformer with GQA, SwiGLU, and RoPE across 28 layers. rsLoRA [14] scales weight updates as Delta_W = (alpha/sqrt(r)) * B*A, maintaining gradient variance independent of rank, enabling r=128 without numerical instability. SimPO [15]: L_SimPO = -E[log sigma(beta/|y_w| * log pi_theta(y_w|x) - beta/|y_l| * log pi_theta(y_l|x) - gamma)], where y_w is a grounded statutory audit and y_l an over-called counterpart. Unlike DPO [16], SimPO eliminates the frozen reference model, halving VRAM requirements.")
H2("C. Retrieval Grounding and Grammar-Constrained Decoding")
BP("Multi-stage RAG [19] over 237 statutory chunks: lexical BM25 (k1=1.5, b=0.75) + dense BGE embeddings [20] via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF; [42]), re-ranked by bge-reranker-v2-m3. XGrammar [21] compiles the statutory JSON schema into a pushdown automaton masking non-admissible logits at every step t: Logits'(w) = Logits(w) if w admissible else -infinity. Guarantees 100% syntactic schema validity without retries.")
